nt to lead so what's the  question we should be asking about  health what is your what is it about  your health that gets in the way of you  living the life you want to lead and if  we understand that person by person we  can actually solve that problem it is  about prescription drug costs yep so  higher or lower under emerge early  prescription drug costs are just a small  part 15 to 20% of the equation we need  to look at the whole cost of care the  whole total therapy what is the best  solution  when sovaldi came out for hepatitis C we  solved that problem by just saying sure  we're gonna pay for it because it's the  other costs associated with Hep C are so  catastrophic it was better for us to get  people treated and cured the concern  with this merger is going back to your  car analogy is that basically what's  gonna happen is people are only gonna  have a choice between two cars and that  the lack of choice the consolidation  means prices might go down a little bit  at first but then you can't go anywhere  else new entrance to the market can't  happen what's the argument what's the  rebuttal there well why would people  only have two choices well because  you're making the the you're offering  them what their choices are so it's  totally up to you they can't they can't  make choices based on price they can  you're the only game in town  we can't force people at the CVS but if  it's the only if it's the only drug  store in town and that's where I'm  getting my health care but there are  plenty of drug stores in town so there's  Walgreens and there are independent  pharmacists and there are a whole host  of other pharmacy welcome say Amazon  getting into the business to compete  with you I think the more competition we  have the better it keeps the system  honest so there's not gonna be any  problem with with so they've they've  just got no case at all in terms of  these fears that there would be too much  consolidation it doesn't make sense for  CVS to turn off all their other  customers just for the 22 million Aetna  customers when they serve over 100  million people today well explain that  integration because what I've read about  what you think that CVS and Aetna can do  to debt together better so in in today's  environment sixty percent of our life  expectancy which has gone down two years  in a row is associated with where we  live 30 percent is our genetic code and  10 percent is the clinical care you  receive so now our zip code matters more  than our genetic code and how long we  love and unless we get into the  community and we begin to pride services  in the community that deal with issues  like social isolation food water  accessibility access if we can do more  of that in the community Inn near  people's homes  then we're gonna win because we're going  to keep people healthy around the issue  that prevents them from living the life  they want to lead so does that mean CBS  gives Aetna the storefront essentially  to do that the storefront becomes a  place call it 10,000 new front doors to  the healthcare system and what we want  to do is provide as many services as we  can in the community to meet social  determinants that's sixty percent versus  waiting for people to show up in the  healthcare system broken and fix them  and a benefit for CVS is I come in to  get a check in on my cold and I'm also  picking up products from CVS when I'm  going there for that other reason no  it's the it's the whole idea that if we  keep people healthier there's lower cost  in the system and when CVS is the  insurer that's an advantage for them  because they're getting the full premium  I get the premium dollar therefore if I  can reduce costs by keeping people  healthier we found this in Medicare  Advantage the more we invest in  individuals around their illnesses and  keeping them away from the health care  system the better off we are Mark  Bertolini be interesting to see these  changes thank you so much thank you yes  this 